Well, they _should_ be able to play nice together, IYAM. In fact, to a
point, I think they do. The main problem is that XL is by far the
superior influence, and skill plays second fiddle, while IMO it should
be the other way around.
After all, IRL, even someone with little talent for, say, music, can
learn to play the piano well enough to bang out an entertaining tune, if
only he practices enough; but a real musical genius will always play
better than him unless the genius has barely played at all, as yet. But
someone who is completely tone deaf (i.e., restricted in music) will
never progress beyond Frere Jacques.

It should be similar in NetHack. Someone who is restricted in a weapon
should barely be able to use it. An expert can be inferior to an @-sign
with basic skill if the latter has XL 20 while the expert has barely
started, but not if the expert has any experience himself. 
In other words, XL and skill should both have an influence on your
proficiency, but skill should have a much greater influence than XL -
the reverse of the current situation. This would solve more than just
the apparent problem - for example, if implemented properly, it would at
last allow Grand Master Monks to use their martial arts to _real_
effect.

